welcome back okay we're talking about  Koopman operator theory why it's useful  for representing nonlinear systems how  you get those representations from data  how you could use those coordinate  transforms for optimal non-linear  control and we've been talking about  kind of what we want to do where it's  going what's hard now and what's you  know what we want to do in the future  and one of the challenges kind of an  essential challenge of modern  data-driven dynamical systems is  handling multi scale physics okay so  assistance with with multiple scales and  space and time very very important to be  able to do this especially with Koopman  operator theory so this is work with  kathleen champion who's working with  nathan cuts and myself on applying these  data-driven methods to multi scale  systems in time and so this is a really  nice use of this Hankel alternative view  of Koopman this kind of time delay  Koopman for multi scale dynamical  systems so she's looked at a number of  systems Vanderpol lawrence Rossler here  these are just single oscillators chosen  parameter values to be quasi periodic  and it turns out that time delay  coordinates and doing DMD dynamic mode  decomposition on time delay coordinates  gives you nearly perfect closed linear  models that capture these kind of  nonlinear essential non-linear dynamics  and Kathleen has since gone on to show  you can use these delay coordinates for  multi scale systems where you have vast  scale separation fast dynamics and slow  dynamics okay so essentially if you  increase the rank of your model of your  havoc model if you increase the kind of  how many eigen time delays you use you  get closer and closer and closer to this  nonlinear van der Pol oscillator and  also the the longer or shorter the  delays you use the closer you get to  these pure-tone sine waves which are  kind of these Koopman embeddings okay  and then like I said she's also applied  this to systems that have a very  different separation of time scale so I  have one  kind of Vanderpol oscillator that's slow  another Vanderpol oscillator that's fast  and they're coupled and essentially what  she's doing in these data-driven Koopman  based methods to pull out to tease out  the timescale separated dynamics from  measurement data so from limited  measurement data in this case she's not  measuring everything she just has x1  plus x2 okay so this is definitely a  step towards moving Koopman methods  towards multi scale systems  Nathan Cutts has also written a paper  called multi resolution DMD where  essentially he uses this multi  resolution in time idea to pull out  these intermittent phenomena like El  Nino modes in the global sea surface  temperature and things like that so you  know very interested in moving towards  multi scale systems with these Koopman  and data Durbin methods this is a very  nice example moving in that direction so  I encourage you to read this paper so  essentially we've been looking at all of  these these topics right when we don't  know equations we try to build these  regression models from data that's what  dynamic mode decomposition extended DMD  sparse Koopman eigenfunction  identification and deep neural nets all  do that when you don't have equations  and they all try to find these  coordinate transformations that map my  nonlinear system into a linear  representation we can use those linear  representations for optimal nonlinear  control so essentially finding those  good coordinate systems is one is a  one-time expensive upfront cost training  that huge neural network or building  those delay coordinates but once I have  that representation I can do optimal  non-linear control much more effectively  using simple linear analysis and then  we've also talked about how to extend  these methods for kind of these extra  difficult problems like chaotic  transient non stationary intermittent  phenomena and multi scale physics okay  and of course this is you know not  complete this is not a complete story  there's so much more to do we need to  tie these together we need to scale  these two much larger systems we need to  figure out where it works and where it  doesn't how fast it is is that upfront  cost  worth what we benefit from downstream  but at least we see that Koopman can  potentially help affect solutions and  all of these major major problems of  modern dynamical systems  ok so we're excited to see where this  goes you know excited to have all of you  working on this and interested in these  problems because this is really a key  enabling technology for the future  taking massive amounts of data and  getting interpretable and effective  coordinate system representations where  those dynamics look linear so that you  can really do powerful things like  nonlinear estimation and prediction and  control okay thank you
 

